## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to combine basic structures (arrays/deque, hashmap, heap) to maintain a sliding window’s frequency distribution and extract top-K on demand.

## Key Concepts
- Sliding window maintenance with a deque
- Frequency counting via a hashmap
- Min-heap with lazy deletions for top-K
- Amortized update and top-K retrieval costs

## Code Example
```javascript
class SlidingWindowTopK {
  constructor(W) {
    this.W = W
    this.window = [] // acts as deque (push is at end, shift from start)
    this.counts = new Map()
    this.heap = [] // min-heap of [freq, value]
  }
  _pushHeap(pair) { /* heap push for [freq, value] */ }
  add(x) {
    this.window.push(x)
    this.counts.set(x, (this.counts.get(x) || 0) + 1)
    // push updated freq
    this._pushHeap([this.counts.get(x), x])
    if (this.window.length > this.W) {
      const y = this.window.shift()
      const f = (this.counts.get(y) || 0) - 1
      if (f <= 0) this.counts.delete(y)
      else this.counts.set(y, f)
      this._pushHeap([f, y])
    }
  }
  topK(K) {
    const res = []
    const seen = new Set()
    const pulled = []
    while (res.length < Math.min(K, this.counts.size) && this.heap.length) {
      const [freq, val] = this._popHeap()
      if (!this.counts.has(val) || this.counts.get(val) !== freq) continue
      if (!seen.has(val)) {
        seen.add(val)
        res.push(val)
        pulled.push([freq, val])
      }
    }
    // restore elements for future calls
    for (const p of pulled) this._pushHeap(p)
    return res
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests practical DP reasoning: variable step costs require deriving both a memoized and a tabular solution, handle unreachable states, and discuss trade-offs.

## Key Concepts

- Top-down DP with memoization
- Bottom-up DP (tabulation)
- Handling unreachable states with Infinity
- Time/space complexity: O(n|S|) time, O(n) space

## Code Example

```javascript
function minCostTopDown(n, steps, memo=new Map()){
  if(n===0) return 0
  if(n<0) return Infinity
  if(memo.has(n)) return memo.get(n)
  let best = Infinity
  for(let s of steps){
    const c = s + minCostTopDown(n-s, steps, memo)
    if(c < best) best = c
  }
  memo.set(n, best)
  return best
}

function minCostBottomUp(n, steps){
  const INF = Number.POSITIVE_INFINITY
  const dp = Array(n+1).fill(INF)
  dp[0] = 0
  for(let i=1; i<=n; i++){
    for(let s of steps){
      if(i>=s && dp[i-s] !== INF){
        dp[i] = Math.min(dp[i], s + dp[i-s])
      }
    }
  }
  return dp[n] === INF ? -1 : dp[n]
}
```

